WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.050
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Welcome to "Get

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Cooking in Cloud," where
we share the best recipes

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.190
to apply in your Cloud kitchen.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.260
I'm Priyanka, and
in today's episode,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.020
we'll share the recipe
to plan for data recovery

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.300
when production environment
is on Google Cloud.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.765
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:25.250
In the last episode, we
learned about Main Street Style

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:28.220
that deployed their production
environment on premises,

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.610
and set up a data recovery
and backup on Google Cloud.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.640
We also learned that there are
two scenarios involved when

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.230
we talk about data recovery.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.290
It's the data backups
and the database backups.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:42.660
Today we will talk about main
event planning, who currently

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.020
runs the infrastructure
on Google Cloud,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:47.360
so the disaster recovery
environment also

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.740
runs on Google Cloud.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:52.160
First let's talk about that
data backup and recovery.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:53.900
They have a tiered
storage pattern

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.050
on Google Cloud with Persistent
Disk attached to Compute

00:00:57.050 --> 00:01:00.410
Engine, and they migrate
data to a low-cost tier

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.780
like nearline or
coldline storage,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.269
since the requirement to
access the backup data

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.240
is very less likely.

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:10.730
Now, let's talk about the
database backups and recovery.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.150
They have one self-managed
MySQL database deployed

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:18.200
on Compute Engine, and they use
Cloud BigTable and BigQuery,

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.430
which are managed databases.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.780
Managed databases are
designed for scale.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.610
BigTable provides
regional replication,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.640
which provides
higher availability

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.580
than a single cluster,
additional read throughputs,

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.130
and a higher durability
and resilience

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.530
in case of zonal failures.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.110
They use BigQuery
to archive data,

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.450
which is a great cost-effective
storage for long-term,

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.570
since the storage
price drops to 50%

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:48.810
if the data does not
change for 90 days.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.690
Best part about
using BigQuery is

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:54.740
that it's replicated by default.
But remember, that does not

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:58.520
save you from data corruption--
say, by erroneous updates.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.680
For more on dealing
with data corruption,

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.860
or if you're using any other
managed Google Cloud databases

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:07.160
like Cloud Spanner, Cloud
Composer, or Datastore,

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:10.020
there's a link in the
description just for you.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:13.910
Now, let's talk about that one
self-managed MySQL database

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.450
that Main Event Planning has
deployed on Compute Engine.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.770
Here's how this works.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.860
Create a VPC network,
and create custom image

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:24.290
that's configured with
the application service.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.990
As a part of the image, make
sure the persistent disk is

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:29.150
attached for the
data being processed,

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.210
create a snapshot from the
attached persistent disk,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.570
and configure a startup script
to create a persistent disk

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.600
from the latest snapshot,
and mount the disk.

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:41.780
Then create an instance template
from the previously created

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:42.620
image.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.900
Using this instance
template, configure

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.650
a regional managed instance
group with a target size of 1.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:50.780
Make sure the health
checks are configured

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.760
at the managed instance groups.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.680
Configure internal
load balancing

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.800
using the regional managed
instance group as well.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.850
Then configure a scheduled task
to create regular snapshots

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:03.560
of the persistent disk.

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.290
Now, what happens in
case of a disaster?

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.300
Well, Main Event
Planning does not

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.580
need to initiate
any failover steps,

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.680
because they occur
automatically.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:15.350
That is the best part of
the default HA features

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.120
available in Google Cloud.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:20.150
In the event a replacement
database instance is needed,

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.460
disk configuration
automatically brings up

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.400
another database server
of the correct version,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.920
attaches a persistent
disk that has the latest

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.440
backup and
transaction log files,

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.110
minimizes the need to
reconfigure clients that

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.300
communicate with
the database server,

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.900
ensures the same Google Cloud
Security controls, the IAM

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:40.730
policies, and the
firewall settings

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.980
apply to the recovered
database server.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.780
To learn more about
these components,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.180
check out the previous
episode on cold disaster

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.990
recovery for applications
on Google Cloud.

00:03:49.990 --> 00:03:51.240
Well, there you have it.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.300
If you have a production
application deployed

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:56.330
in Google Cloud and need to
set up data recovery, then

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:58.010
hopefully you've
learned some strategies

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.500
to apply in your
specific scenario.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.870
That's all for today on
"Get Cooking in Cloud."

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:06.690
Here's hoping you're whipping
up your own DR strategy.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.930
Join us next time, where
